how much alcohol we can safely consume  is being drastically rethought new  recommended guidelines for Canadians  advise even moderate drinking anything  more than two drinks a week puts your  health at risk the update is from the  Canadian Center on substance use and  addiction which reviewed six thousand  studies on alcohol use it defines a  standard drink as 12 ounces of beer 5  ounces of wine or one and a half ounces  of spirits according to the report  consuming no alcohol has significant  benefits to health and sleep three to  six drinks per week puts you at moderate  risk of developing seven types of cancer  including colon and breast cancer and  more than seven alcoholic drinks a week  puts you in increasingly high risk of  heart disease and stroke Mike droulet on  the new thinking about the risks of  drinking  whether it's at a bar a sporting event  or party alcohol isn't merely prevalent  it's celebrated in society today a  decade ago the medical community put a  number on what moderation looks like no  more than 15 drinks per week for men 10  for women but now after a review of 6  000 medical studies the new recommended  guideline is no more than two drinks we  were also very shocked about how  um how much different and how far we've  come in the science since 2011.  and um you know frankly we're like wow  like how you know how are we going to  get people to drink two drinks a week  how indeed the Canadian Center on  substance use and addiction wants  warning labels placed on bottles  informing consumers of the increased  risk for cancer heart disease and stroke  so after seven drinks the risk is  exponential  rate than it does at the moderate level  France has had labels on its bottles  since 1991. yet a recent study found  that they were too small and lacked  information in short they were  ineffective Canada's wine industry has  another idea labels but with more focus  on information than warnings uh 12 or 10  percent to alcohol bottle wine is going  to happen obviously a lesser amount of  alcohol than a 15 uh alcohol so uh  putting that on the label and  understanding what a standard breakiness  makes total sense to us now the  recommendations are just that  recommendations which helped Canada is  in the process of reviewing in the  meantime from a long-term medical point  of view the news isn't all Grim if  you've been drinking really heavily for  the last decade and we're very unaware  of these risks there is the potential  the damage isn't necessarily done it's a  dramatic shift from previous reports  well Canadians listen  Mike drolet Global News Toronto
 

